{"id": "EUVD-2026-67962", "enisaUuid": "ca4d1e78-b54a-3ff9-a9f8-140fe583b199", "description": "IGEL OS 12 before 12.9.0, 12.8.3 LTS and IGEL OS 11 before 11.11.150 contain a secure boot bypass vulnerability in the GRUB boot stage that allows physically present attackers to gain unauthorized root access by placing an unsigned empty file named igel.conf on a partition. Attackers can exploit GRUB's fail-open signature verification behavior to drop into an interactive GRUB prompt, then boot the device's own kernel with additional command-line arguments to obtain a root shell with the disk unlocked while leaving TPM PCR values unaltered.", "datePublished": "2026-08-28T21:11:24", "dateUpdated": "2026-10-01T15:21:40", "baseScore": 6.8, "baseScoreVersion": "4.0", "baseScoreVector": "CVSS:4.0/AV:P/AC:L/AT:N/PR:N/UI:N/VC:H/VI:H/VA:N/SC:N/SI:N/SA:N", "references": ["https://kb.igel.com/en/security-safety/current/isn-2026-20-grub-shell-escape-in-igel-os", "https://blog.amberwolf.com/blog/2026/august/thin-client-thin-crypto-overview/", "https://media.defcon.org/DEF%20CON%2034/DEF%20CON%2034%20presentations/DEF%20CON%2034%20presentations/DEF%20CON%2034%20-%20Darren%20McDonald%20-%20Thin%20Client%20Thin%20Crypto%20-%20Bypassing%20Full-Desk%20Encryption%20Across%20Three%20Major%20Thin%20Clients%20Vendors%20without%20Breaking%20a%20Ci.pdf", "https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/igel-os-12-11-secure-boot-bypass-via-unsigned-igel-conf-file"], "aliases": ["CVE-2026-82018", "GHSA-7c38-49cx-fjvw"], "assigner": "VulnCheck", "epss": 0.25, "enisaIdProduct": [{"id": "2892ca51-e4d6-37bb-b534-5eba148b9e34", "product": {"name": "IGEL OS 12", "vendor": {"name": "IGEL"}}, "product_version": "patch: 12.8.3"}, {"id": "51259498-d728-3620-ba82-7f663517428f", "product": {"name": "IGEL OS 12", "vendor": {"name": "IGEL"}}, "product_version": "patch: 12.9.0"}, {"id": "54411541-2d76-3460-84e4-bb354e63808b", "product": {"name": "IGEL OS 11", "vendor": {"name": "IGEL"}}, "product_version": "11.0.0 <11.11.15